export function algorithms(add,cpp) {
const scan="std::size_t inspections(std::span<const int> values, int key) {\n    std::size_t count = 0;\n    for (int value : values) {\n        ++count;\n        if (value == key)\n            break;\n    }\n    return count;\n}";
add('complexity','count-work','Choose an input size and a unit of work',
'A complexity statement begins with a problem size and a measured operation. For a linear scan, let n be the number of live candidate elements and count comparisons with the key. Compiler startup and printing are separate costs.\n\nAn absent key forces this implementation to inspect the whole range. A first-position hit answers a different case and should not be substituted into a worst-case claim.',
cpp('array iostream span',"    const std::array<int, 4> values{2, 4, 6, 8};\n    std::cout << \"absent=\" << inspections(values, 7)\n              << \" first=\" << inspections(values, 2) << \"\\n\";",scan),'absent=4 first=1',
'The same four-element range produces four comparisons for absence and one for the first-position hit.',
'Elapsed compilation time does not measure the algorithm’s comparison count.',
['What is n in this model?','The number of candidate elements','The executable byte size','The number of printed characters'],'The search problem grows with the number of candidate elements.',
'Double the range with an absent key and predict the inspection count before running. Keep setup and output outside any later timed region.');
add('complexity','growth-bounds','Distinguish upper bounds from tight bounds',
'Big O gives an asymptotic upper bound; Big Omega gives a lower bound; Big Theta gives both up to constant factors. Name the counted function before giving its growth class.\n\nFor f(n)=3n+2 and n at least one, 3n <= f(n) <= 5n. The algebra proves a tight linear bound; a printed table only illustrates it.',
cpp('iostream',"    for (unsigned n : {1u, 2u, 4u})\n        std::cout << n << \":\" << 3 * n + 2 << \" <= \" << 5 * n << \"\\n\";"),'1:5 <= 5\n2:8 <= 10\n4:14 <= 20',
'All printed values satisfy the stated upper bound. The inequality, rather than the finite sample, covers arbitrary n in the mathematical model.',
'O(n) does not mean exactly n operations, and Big O is not itself a synonym for worst case.',
['What is the tight growth class of 3n+2?','Theta(n)','Theta(n squared)','Theta(1)'],'Both linear upper and lower bounds hold beyond a fixed threshold.',
'Write a proof of the two inequalities for every n>=1. Explain why O(n squared) is also true but less informative.');
add('complexity','growth-comparison','Compare doubling behavior',
'Repeated halving yields logarithmic growth. A pairwise nested traversal yields quadratic work. Combining linear work across logarithmically many levels gives a common n log n model.\n\nThese expressions can count different kinds of work, so compare growth behavior rather than treating all model values as interchangeable elapsed times.',
cpp('iostream',"    for (unsigned n : {4u, 8u}) {\n        unsigned levels = 0;\n        for (unsigned m = n; m > 1; m /= 2)\n            ++levels;\n        std::cout << n << \" log=\" << levels << \" linear=\" << n << \" square=\" << n * n\n                  << \"\\n\";\n    }"),'4 log=2 linear=4 square=16\n8 log=3 linear=8 square=64',
'Doubling n adds one halving level, doubles the linear count, and quadruples the square.',
'A logarithmic search still needs its ordering and access preconditions.',
['How does n squared change when n doubles?','It quadruples','It doubles','It adds one'],'The square of 2n is 4n squared.',
'Extend the table to 16 and 32. Explain why n log n combines the number of levels with work per level.');
add('complexity','amortized-append','Amortized cost describes an operation sequence',
'An abstract growing buffer that doubles capacity copies 1+2+4+... elements at growth points. Across N appends that total is linear even though a particular append can copy every existing element.\n\nThis is amortized analysis over a sequence, not an average over randomly distributed inputs. The example models a chosen doubling policy; std::vector need not use exactly that factor.',
cpp('iostream',"    unsigned capacity = 0, copied = 0;\n    for (unsigned length = 0; length < 9; ++length)\n        if (length == capacity) {\n            copied += length;\n            capacity = capacity ? capacity * 2 : 1;\n        }\n    std::cout << \"appends=9 copied=\" << copied << \" capacity=\" << capacity << \"\\n\";"),'appends=9 copied=15 capacity=16',
'The ninth append triggers copying eight elements, bringing the sequence total to fifteen.',
'Amortized constant time does not make every individual append constant time.',
['Can one append still cost linear work?','Yes, at a growth boundary','No, amortized forbids it','Only for random inputs'],'The sequence bound permits occasional expensive operations.',
'Calculate the total after eight appends and compare it with nine. Prove an upper bound in terms of the final length.');
add('complexity','benchmark-contract','Make a prediction before collecting observations',
'A benchmark should answer a specified workload question after correctness checks pass. For absent-key linear scans, doubling the candidate range predicts twice as many key comparisons.\n\nOperation counts isolate the algorithm from timing noise. Later timing should keep parsing, allocation, output, and compiler startup outside the measured region and retain raw observations.',
cpp('array iostream span',"    const std::array<int, 8> values{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8};\n    auto small = inspections(std::span(values).first(4), 99);\n    auto large = inspections(values, 99);\n    std::cout << small << \" \" << large << \" ratio=\" << large / small << \"\\n\";",scan),'4 8 ratio=2',
'The instrumented implementation matches the stated absent-key prediction on these two inputs; the samples are checks rather than an asymptotic proof.',
'Changing target distribution changes the benchmark’s question.',
['What should precede timing?','Correctness checks and a specified workload','Choosing the fastest-looking sample','Removing all boundary cases'],'Measurement is interpretable only when the workload and result contract are clear.',
'Add first-position hits as a separately labeled workload. Explain why combining the cases can hide the growth you intended to study.');

const lower="std::size_t lower(std::span<const int> values, int key) {\n    std::size_t first = 0, last = values.size();\n    while (first < last) {\n        auto middle = first + (last - first) / 2;\n        if (values[middle] < key)\n            first = middle + 1;\n        else\n            last = middle;\n    }\n    return first;\n}";
add('searching','linear-search-result','Represent a missing search result explicitly',
'A search result must distinguish a found position from absence. optional<size_t> avoids assigning a negative sentinel to an unsigned index or confusing position zero with failure.\n\nThe scan works on unsorted input and returns the first match. Those behaviors belong to its contract independently of how it counts or stores candidates.',
cpp('array iostream optional span',"    const std::array<int, 4> values{8, 3, 8, 1};\n    auto found = find(values, 8);\n    if (found)\n        std::cout << \"first=\" << *found << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"missing=\" << !find(values, 9) << \"\\n\";","std::optional<std::size_t> find(std::span<const int> values, int key) {\n    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < values.size(); ++i)\n        if (values[i] == key)\n            return i;\n    return std::nullopt;\n}"),'first=0\nmissing=1',
'The first match is a present optional containing zero. The absent key produces no position.',
'Testing the contained index as a Boolean would treat the first element as a failure.',
['Is a found position of zero a success?','Yes','No','Only in sorted arrays'],'Presence and numeric index value are different concepts.',
'Test empty input, duplicates, and a last-position hit. State whether a future index implementation must preserve first-match behavior.');
add('searching','binary-search-invariant','Maintain a half-open candidate interval',
'Binary search maintains a half-open interval of possible answers. lower_bound searches for the first position whose value is not less than the key, so its answer can equal size when every element is smaller.\n\nCompute the midpoint as first+(last-first)/2 to avoid adding two potentially large indices. The proof depends on the input’s ordering.',
cpp('array iostream span',"    const std::array<int, 5> values{1, 3, 3, 5, 9};\n    std::cout << lower(values, 3) << \" \" << lower(values, 4) << \" \" << lower(values, 10)\n              << \"\\n\";",lower),'1 3 5',
'The first 3 is at position one, 4 would be inserted at position three, and 10 belongs at the end.',
'A valid insertion position is not proof that the key is present; compare the element only when the position is in range.',
['Can lower return values.size()?','Yes, for an insertion at the end','No, it always finds an element','Only for invalid input'],'The boundary position is a valid search answer.',
'Trace first and last for empty input and for a key below every element. Explain why the interval shrinks each iteration.');
add('searching','duplicate-range','Find the full range of equal keys',
'lower_bound and upper_bound bracket equal keys in a sorted range. Their difference counts matches without scanning every equal element. A closed equality query can therefore be expressed using two half-open boundary searches.\n\nBoth operations must use ordering compatible with the data. A search by a different projected field needs a separately appropriate index.',
cpp('algorithm iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{1, 3, 3, 3, 8};\n    auto first = std::lower_bound(values.begin(), values.end(), 3);\n    auto last = std::upper_bound(values.begin(), values.end(), 3);\n    std::cout << \"matches=\" << last - first << \"\\n\";"),'matches=3',
'The lower iterator points to the first 3 and the upper iterator points to 8, excluding that element from the equal range.',
'Treating upper_bound as another pointer to the final equal element introduces an off-by-one error.',
['Is the upper boundary included in the equal range?','No','Yes','Only if it equals end'],'The result is the half-open interval [first,last).',
'Test a missing key, all-equal input, and a key at the final position. Check the count against a linear reference.');
add('searching','partition-point','Search a monotone predicate',
'Binary search generalizes to a range partitioned by a predicate: all true results precede all false results. partition_point locates that boundary without requiring the predicate to be equality with a key.\n\nThe monotonicity condition is essential. An arbitrary filter predicate scattered through the range does not satisfy the search contract.',
cpp('algorithm iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{2, 4, 6, 9, 11};\n    auto boundary = std::partition_point(values.begin(), values.end(),\n                                         [](int value) { return value < 8; });\n    std::cout << \"prefix=\" << boundary - values.begin() << \" next=\" << *boundary\n              << \"\\n\";"),'prefix=3 next=9',
'Exactly three elements satisfy the predicate before the first false result.',
'A predicate that alternates true and false violates partition_point’s precondition even if the input happens to be sorted for another purpose.',
['What ordering of predicate results is required?','A true prefix followed by a false suffix','Alternating true and false','All results must be false'],'The algorithm searches one monotone boundary.',
'Create an all-true and an all-false fixture. Handle boundary==end before dereferencing it.');
add('searching','index-tradeoffs','Account for building and maintaining an index',
'An index accelerates some lookups by storing additional structure that must be built and maintained. A sorted vector permits binary search but insertion may shift a suffix. A hash index has different ordering and worst-case guarantees.\n\nThis fixture shows a sorted insertion position and the resulting sequence. It does not claim that every indexed workload is faster than scanning.',
cpp('algorithm iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> index{1, 4, 9};\n    auto position = std::lower_bound(index.begin(), index.end(), 5);\n    auto shifted = index.end() - position;\n    index.insert(position, 5);\n    std::cout << \"shifted=\" << shifted << \"\\n\";\n    for (int key : index)\n        std::cout << key;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";"),'shifted=1\n1459',
'Only the old final element lies after the insertion position. The vector retains sorted order after insertion.',
'Quoting only lookup complexity hides index build costs, updates, memory, and workload selectivity.',
['Which additional cost matters for a sorted vector?','Shifting elements during insertion','Every search sorts the entire vector','The key must be dynamically allocated'],'Maintaining order has a mutation cost.',
'Compare batches of many lookups with batches of many insertions. State which costs belong in the workload model.');

add('sorting-strategies','insertion-sort','Insertion sort maintains a sorted prefix',
'Insertion sort takes the next element and moves it into an already sorted prefix. Its invariant is that elements before the current index are ordered and contain exactly the earlier input values.\n\nThe worst-case number of shifts is quadratic, but nearly sorted small inputs can require little movement. A correct implementation keeps the temporarily removed key in an owning local value.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{4, 1, 3, 2};\n    unsigned shifts = 0;\n    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < values.size(); ++i) {\n        int key = values[i];\n        auto j = i;\n        while (j > 0 && key < values[j - 1]) {\n            values[j] = values[j - 1];\n            --j;\n            ++shifts;\n        }\n        values[j] = key;\n    }\n    for (int value : values)\n        std::cout << value;\n    std::cout << \" shifts=\" << shifts << \"\\n\";"),'1234 shifts=4',
'The key is preserved while larger prefix values shift right. Four shifts are needed for this particular input.',
'Using an unsigned index without testing j>0 before reading j-1 can underflow the index.',
['What is sorted before iteration i?','The prefix [0,i)','The entire suffix','Only the final two elements'],'Each iteration extends the sorted prefix by one element.',
'Count shifts for sorted and reversed inputs of the same size. Verify duplicate values are neither lost nor invented.');
add('sorting-strategies','merge-ranges','Merge two sorted ranges',
'Merging repeatedly selects the smaller next element from two sorted inputs. The result remains ordered because each remaining input suffix is ordered. This operation is the linear-work step used by merge sort.\n\nThe destination owns the output values. Its capacity or insertion interface must support the complete combined length.',
cpp('algorithm iostream iterator vector',"    std::vector<int> left{1, 4, 8}, right{2, 4, 9}, result;\n    std::merge(left.begin(), left.end(), right.begin(), right.end(),\n               std::back_inserter(result));\n    for (int value : result)\n        std::cout << value;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";"),'124489',
'Both copies of 4 are retained. The merge consumes each input once and appends six output elements.',
'Merging unsorted inputs does not repair their ordering automatically.',
['What does merge require of each input?','It is already sorted under the same ordering','It has unique values','It has the same length as the other input'],'The correctness argument relies on ordered remaining suffixes.',
'Test an empty side and equal values on both sides. Label equal records to investigate merge’s stability rule.');
add('sorting-strategies','stable-order','Preserve equivalent-record order when the contract needs it',
'A stable sort preserves the input order among elements equivalent under its comparator. This matters when a previous ordering or arrival sequence is part of the observable result.\n\nStability is distinct from defining a tie-breaker. A total tie-breaker specifies a new ordering; stability retains the earlier ordering of equivalent elements.',
cpp('algorithm iostream string vector',"    std::vector<Row> rows{{2, \"A\"}, {1, \"B\"}, {2, \"C\"}};\n    std::stable_sort(rows.begin(), rows.end(),\n                     [](const Row &a, const Row &b) { return a.key < b.key; });\n    for (const auto &row : rows)\n        std::cout << row.label;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";","struct Row {\n    int key;\n    std::string label;\n};"),'BAC',
'B moves before the key-2 group. A remains before C because their keys are equivalent.',
'std::sort is not required to preserve the order of equivalent records.',
['Why does A remain before C?','The sort is stable and their keys are equivalent','All sorts compare labels automatically','The labels are pointers'],'Stability preserves their input-relative order.',
'Reverse the two key-2 records before sorting and compare. Then replace stability with an explicit label tie-breaker and explain the difference.');
add('sorting-strategies','selection-topk','Select a rank without fully sorting',
'nth_element partitions a range so that the chosen position contains an element of its sorted rank. Elements before it are not greater than elements after it under the ordering, but each side is not necessarily sorted.\n\nThis supports median and top-k operations when a complete order would do unnecessary work. Sort only the selected prefix if its displayed order is part of the output contract.',
cpp('algorithm iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{9, 1, 7, 3, 5};\n    auto middle = values.begin() + 2;\n    std::nth_element(values.begin(), middle, values.end());\n    std::cout << \"median=\" << *middle << \"\\n\";"),'median=5',
'Position two has the median of five values. The program intentionally does not print unspecified internal ordering of the partitions.',
'Printing the whole range as though nth_element sorted it makes an unsupported assumption.',
['Are both partitions fully sorted?','No','Yes','Only when all values are distinct'],'The operation establishes a rank partition, not complete sorting.',
'Select the three smallest values, sort that prefix for display, and compare it with an independently sorted copy.');
add('sorting-strategies','sort-oracle','Check ordering and permutation preservation independently',
'A sort must produce an ordered sequence and preserve the input multiset. Checking only orderedness would accept a result that discarded every element. An independent reference result can expose both kinds of failure.\n\nUse adversarial fixtures with duplicates and negative values, then broaden the workload without replacing the contract with the implementation’s own logic.',
cpp('algorithm iostream vector',"    const std::vector<int> input{3, -1, 3, 0};\n    auto result = input;\n    std::ranges::sort(result);\n    const std::vector<int> expected{-1, 0, 3, 3};\n    bool correct = std::ranges::is_sorted(result) && result == expected;\n    std::cout << \"verified=\" << correct << \"\\n\";\n    return correct ? 0 : 1;"),'verified=1',
'The expected sequence specifies all four values and duplicate multiplicity, rather than just a monotonicity property.',
'A test oracle that calls the same buggy custom sort can agree with it for the wrong reason.',
['Would an empty output satisfy is_sorted?','Yes, so size and content also matter','No','Only for empty input'],'Orderedness alone does not establish permutation preservation.',
'Check empty, single, all-equal, already sorted, and reversed fixtures. Compare a custom implementation with the standard algorithm.');

add('recursion','recursive-progress','A recursive call needs a smaller problem',
'A recursive definition needs a base case and a progress measure that reaches it. Summing the first n positive integers reduces n by one until zero. The return combines the solved smaller problem with the current contribution.\n\nStack usage grows with recursion depth. Keep this teaching input small and do not generalize it into a safe interface for arbitrary unsigned values.',
cpp('iostream',"    std::cout << sum(4) << \"\\n\";","unsigned sum(unsigned n) {\n    return n == 0 ? 0 : n + sum(n - 1);\n}"),'10',
'The calls reach sum(0), then combine 1, 2, 3, and 4 while returning.',
'A missing base case or a call that does not decrease the measure can recurse until stack exhaustion.',
['Which measure decreases?','n','The final sum','The return address'],'Each non-base call uses n-1.',
'Trace the calls for zero and one. Add a documented input bound that keeps both arithmetic and stack use acceptable.');
add('recursion','divide-conquer','Split a range and combine subproblem results',
'Divide-and-conquer separates a problem into smaller ranges, solves them, and combines the answers. A span can express each borrowed subrange without copying its elements.\n\nThe recursion must handle an empty range and a single element before splitting. Ownership stays in the caller’s array throughout the computation.',
cpp('array iostream span',"    const std::array<int, 5> values{2, 3, 4, 5, 6};\n    std::cout << total(values) << \"\\n\";","int total(std::span<const int> values) {\n    if (values.empty())\n        return 0;\n    if (values.size() == 1)\n        return values.front();\n    auto middle = values.size() / 2;\n    return total(values.first(middle)) + total(values.subspan(middle));\n}"),'20',
'The five-element span splits into disjoint prefixes and suffixes whose sums combine to twenty.',
'Overlapping slices can double-count elements; a gap can silently omit them.',
['Do these spans own copied elements?','No, they borrow disjoint ranges','Yes','Only the right span owns data'],'The original array owns all elements until the calls finish.',
'Instrument visits and confirm each element contributes once. Compare recursion depth with a linear recursive summation.');
add('recursion','memoization','Cache results for repeated subproblems',
'Naive Fibonacci recursion revisits the same subproblems. Memoization stores each computed result so later calls reuse it. The cache changes work from an exponential recursion tree to a linear number of distinct states.\n\nCache size and arithmetic range are separate limits. This example uses a fixed small state space with optional entries to distinguish uncomputed from a real zero.',
cpp('array iostream optional',"    std::array<std::optional<unsigned>, 11> memo{};\n    std::cout << fib(10, memo) << \"\\n\";","unsigned fib(unsigned n, std::array<std::optional<unsigned>, 11> &memo) {\n    if (n < 2)\n        return n;\n    if (memo.at(n))\n        return *memo[n];\n    memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);\n    return *memo[n];\n}"),'55',
'Every non-base state through ten is computed once and then reused. The optional remains engaged even when a cached value could be zero.',
'A cache indexed by unvalidated external n can exceed its supported state space.',
['What is being cached?','Results of distinct subproblems','Every stack frame forever','Only the final printed string'],'Repeated calls reuse the stored result for a state.',
'Count newly computed states and cache hits separately. Reject n beyond the declared table size before recursion.');
add('recursion','tabulation','Choose an order that makes dependencies available',
'Bottom-up dynamic programming fills states in an order where prerequisites are already computed. For Fibonacci, state n depends only on the previous two states, allowing constant auxiliary storage.\n\nReducing storage does not remove the need for a bounded numeric domain. Checked arithmetic is still required when the requested state can exceed the result type.',
cpp('iostream',"    unsigned previous = 0, current = 1;\n    for (unsigned n = 0; n < 10; ++n) {\n        auto next = previous + current;\n        previous = current;\n        current = next;\n    }\n    std::cout << previous << \"\\n\";"),'55',
'After ten updates, previous holds F(10). Only two preceding values are retained.',
'Updating previous before computing next from both old values can destroy a dependency.',
['Why compute next first?','Both old state values are still needed','It allocates the full table','It prevents all overflow'],'The temporary preserves the recurrence’s inputs until the new state is known.',
'Compare against the memoized implementation for every n from zero to ten. Add a checked-addition failure path.');
add('recursion','backtracking-state','Undo tentative state before exploring a sibling',
'Backtracking explores a choice, recursively explores its consequences, and restores the state before trying another choice. Ownership of the mutable path should be clear so sibling branches do not inherit one another’s tentative additions.\n\nA small subset enumeration makes the restore step visible. More complex solvers also need pruning rules and a documented output order.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> path;\n    subsets(1, 2, path);","void subsets(int next, int limit, std::vector<int> &path) {\n    if (next > limit) {\n        for (int value : path)\n            std::cout << value;\n        std::cout << \".\\n\";\n        return;\n    }\n    subsets(next + 1, limit, path);\n    path.push_back(next);\n    subsets(next + 1, limit, path);\n    path.pop_back();\n}"),'.\n2.\n1.\n12.',
'The exclude branch runs before the include branch. pop_back restores the caller’s path after exploring each included choice.',
'Forgetting the undo step makes later branches observe stale choices.',
['What must hold when a recursive call returns?','The caller’s path is restored','The path contains every explored element','The path is permanently sorted'],'Each call restores the mutation it introduced before returning.',
'Record the path size on entry and verify it is unchanged on return. Add a pruning rule for a bounded subset sum.');
}
